# Target C-II — one-sign TB1 layers are disjoint on the active-diameter side

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the one-sign side of the strong-TB1 branch through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** TB1 common-axis cone collapse; host transverse margin; exact layer support; contact-swap theorem; canonical contact-minimal then diameter-pair-minimal normalization  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_active_diameter_decoupling_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The one-sign contact-swap argument was previously local: at each strong-TB1 layer, every switching actual diameter shortens under the exact contact swap. The common-axis theorem implies a stronger global statement.

An actual diameter cannot switch at **two different one-sign strong-TB1 layers**.

Indeed, if the same diameter pair moves under two disjoint path-layer translations, its two inward incidence vectors are exact negatives. But every one-sign layer requires its inward vector to have a fixed positive projection on the local swap axis, while all swap axes differ by only `O(D^-1)`. The two requirements would force an order-`D` change in projection across axes whose difference has norm only `O(D^-1)`.

Thus the active-diameter switching sets of all one-sign TB1 layers are pairwise disjoint as sets of diameter pairs.

This makes a genuine simultaneous finite move possible. Thin the one-sign layer indices by parity so no two retained layers are adjacent. Apply the exact contact swap on **every retained layer at once**. Then

- every old unit contact remains exactly unit;
- every old actual diameter is affected by at most one layer;
- every affected old actual diameter strictly shortens.

Hence all coupling in this simultaneous move comes from previously strict source pairs. Canonical minimality forces at least one inactive pair to become invalid or active. On the current positive-density one-sign branch, the simultaneous exact-swap family may be chosen with density more than

\[
\boxed{
{s_0\over4}D-o(D)
=(6.1796875\times10^{-7})D-o(D).
}
\]

The strong-TB1 active constraints therefore do **not** form the long-range coupling left in C-II. After parity thinning, the entire old active system separates coordinatewise; only inactive lower/upper slack can obstruct the linear simultaneous swap.

---

## 1. Common swap-axis control

At every one-sign strong-TB1 interface `i`, let

\[
q_i=|a_i+b_i|,
\qquad
w_i={a_i+b_i\over q_i},
\qquad
v_i=-q_iw_i
\tag{AD.1}
\]

be the exact contact-swap data.

The cone-collapse theorem supplies one common oriented transverse axis `w_*` and a fixed constant `C` such that, uniformly over the robust strong-TB1 population,

\[
|w_i-w_*|\le {C+o(1)\over D}.
\tag{AD.2}
\]

Hence for every two such interfaces

\[
\boxed{
|w_i-w_j|\le {2C+o(1)\over D}.
}
\tag{AD.3}
\]

The contact-swap theorem and the host transverse margin give, on every one-sign interface, every switching actual diameter `h` an orientation-independent inward vector `n_{h,i}` with

\[
|n_{h,i}|=D
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
n_{h,i}\cdot w_i\ge(c_H-o(1))D,
}
\tag{AD.4}
\]

where

\[
c_H=0.453819885927\ldots>0.
\]

---

## 2. One actual diameter cannot switch at two one-sign layers

Suppose an actual diameter pair `h=xy` switched at two distinct one-sign path layers `R_i,R_j`.

The layers are disjoint. Since `h` moves under the translation of a layer exactly when one endpoint lies in that layer, switching at both `i,j` forces one endpoint of `h` into `R_i` and the other into `R_j`. Therefore its two orientation-independent inward vectors are exact negatives:

\[
\boxed{
n_{h,j}=-n_{h,i}.}
\tag{AD.5}
\]

Put `n=n_{h,i}`. Applying (AD.4) at the two interfaces gives

\[
n\cdot w_i\ge(c_H-o(1))D,
\]

and

\[
(-n)\cdot w_j\ge(c_H-o(1))D,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
n\cdot(w_i-w_j)\ge(2c_H-o(1))D.}
\tag{AD.6}
\]

On the other hand, (AD.3) and `|n|=D` give

\[
|n\cdot(w_i-w_j)|
\le D|w_i-w_j|
\le2C+o(1).
\tag{AD.7}
\]

The right side is bounded while (AD.6) tends linearly to infinity. Contradiction for all sufficiently large `D`.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
X_i\cap X_j=\varnothing
\quad\text{for distinct one-sign strong-TB1 interfaces }i,j,
}
\tag{AD.8}
\]

where `X_i` is the moving/switching old actual-diameter set of layer `R_i`.

This conclusion uses the **oriented** common transverse axis. Projective phase collapse alone would not suffice.

---

## 3. Parity thinning removes contact interference

A simultaneous translation of adjacent source layers would make their shared boundary contact receive the difference of two swap vectors, so local exact-swap identities cannot simply be superposed on adjacent layers.

This is removed at constant cost. The selected path layers have integer indices. Split the one-sign population by index parity and keep the larger class `I`. Then

\[
|I|\ge {1\over2}|M_D|,
\tag{AD.9}
\]

and no two indices in `I` are consecutive.

On the `NEAR-ACTIVE` winner of the strong-TB1 sign split,

\[
|M_D|>{s_0\over2}D-o(D),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
|I|>{s_0\over4}D-o(D)
=(6.1796875\times10^{-7})D-o(D).
}
\tag{AD.10}

Translate every source vertex in `R_i`, `i in I`, by its exact swap vector `v_i` from (AD.1), leaving all other vertices fixed.

---

## 4. Every old contact remains exactly unit

The audited stopped-train/cutset decomposition gives exact interval support to every old contact. In particular a contact edge crosses exactly its own selected train, so its two endpoint layers are consecutive along the selected path.

Because `I` contains no consecutive layer indices, an old contact has at most one endpoint in a moved layer.

- If neither endpoint lies in a moved layer, the contact is unchanged.
- If one endpoint lies in `R_i`, its contact belongs to one of the two boundary families of that layer. The local exact-swap identity gives
  \[
  a_i+v_i=-b_i
  \quad\text{or}\quad
  b_i+v_i=-a_i,
  \]
  so the new contact still has length exactly one.

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\text{all old unit contacts remain exactly unit under the simultaneous move.}
}
\tag{AD.11}

No infinitesimal approximation is used.

---

## 5. Every old actual diameter is unchanged or strictly shortened

Take any old actual diameter `h`.

If neither endpoint belongs to a moved layer, it is unchanged.

If one endpoint belongs to some `R_i`, `i in I`, then `h in X_i`. By (AD.8), its other endpoint cannot lie in any other one-sign moved layer. Hence the simultaneous displacement of its relative vector is **exactly the same** as in the single-layer exact swap at `i`.

The one-sign contact-swap theorem therefore gives

\[
|h|_{\rm new}<D.
\tag{AD.12}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\text{every old actual diameter is unchanged or strictly shortened.}
}
\tag{AD.13}
\]

At least one old diameter is shortened for every nonempty `I`, because each strong-TB1 interface has a switching certificate diameter.

---

## 6. Only inactive slack can block the simultaneous swap

Assume, for contradiction, that after the simultaneous exact swaps

- every previously strict noncontact still has distance greater than one, and
- every previously strict nondiameter pair still has distance less than `D`.

Then (AD.11) preserves the entire old contact set, while (AD.13) preserves the diameter upper bound and strictly removes at least one old diameter pair.

If some unaffected old diameter remains, the new source is another diameter-`D` global minimizer with the same number of contacts but fewer diameter pairs. If none remains, the whole source has diameter less than `D`. Both alternatives contradict the canonical normalization/global minimality exactly as in the one-layer contact-swap theorem.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\text{some previously strict source pair obstructs the simultaneous swap.}
}
\tag{AD.14}

A strict pair is moved only through the translations of the layers containing its endpoints, so at most two swap vectors contribute to its relative displacement. With

\[
q_i=O(D^{-1})
\]

uniformly, its total displacement is `O(D^-1)`. Hence every simultaneous obstruction again lies in an inverse-`D` inactive window around distance `1` or distance `D`.

The point is not a better slack constant. It is the separation of roles:

\[
\boxed{
\text{old active diameters are fully decoupled;
all multi-interface coupling is inactive-slack coupling.}
}
\tag{AD.15}

---

## 7. Frontier effect

The one-sign strong-TB1 branch now admits a linear-dimensional finite exact-swap pattern with no active-diameter interaction after one parity thinning:

```text
> (s0/4) D nonadjacent one-sign layers
+ exact contact swap on every retained layer

-> every old contact stays exactly unit
-> every old actual diameter is touched by at most one coordinate
-> every touched old diameter strictly shortens
-> canonical obstruction must come from a previously strict pair
-> every such pair couples at most two retained coordinates.
```

This converts the remaining one-sign TB1 problem into a sparse inactive-constraint system of arity at most two over a linear family of finite swap coordinates.

The next target is therefore combinatorial/finite rather than local KKT geometry: exploit the one- or two-coordinate support of every inactive obstruction to choose a nonzero subset of exact swaps avoiding all lower/upper violations, or prove that any obstruction system blocking all such subsets forces the ultra-near-unit repeated pattern isolated in the repeated-witness theorem.

**Disposition:** proved active-diameter injectivity across one-sign strong-TB1 interfaces and a linear simultaneous exact-swap family whose only blockers are previously strict pairs. C-II and Level I remain open.